column31:
The new edit mode internally will create a copy of an existing product to allow users to make changes to that product, without touching (and altering) the current product. This is necessary as the current product might be still live in the shop and orders for that product need production plans etc. of the currently active product.

For this purpose we need to alter our entity structure and decouple a few properties from the {{Product}} entity. These properties are:

* {{AssignedProducers}}
* {{ProcessingDeadline}}
* {{ProductionStates}}
* {{ProductionSteps}}
* {{ProductionPlans}}

Those properties should be encapsulated in a new entity which has a 1:n relationship to the product (1 product multiple instances for that new entity). 

This new entity probably need some additional properties like {{status}} to track if those information belong to the currently active product, the updated version of that product or an old version of that product.

When searching for a developer i cannot search for firstname + lastname: 

{quote}So ‘Nadine’ and ‘Kleiss’ work, but ‘Nadine Kleiss’, ‘Nadain K’ do not{quote}

Instead it is searchable by lastname + firstname, which is kinda confusing as the table displays developer information as firstname + lastname. 

The filter for this search currently looks like this {{dev.Surname + " " + dev.Name}} and needs to be changed to {{dev.Name + " " + dev.Surname}}

column324:
04/Jun/24 1:12 PM;5ab3ac38d7282b2a6b868d7a;To cancel a order via API, make a PATCH request to {{http://sw6.local/api/order/{id}}} with the following data:

*PATCH - http://sw6.local/api/*

{code:json}{
  "data": {
    "stateId": "b954abeca73947d4b7f3e7ad3534e381" // cancelled
  }
}{code}

The stateId can be obtained from the API aswell (though should be also fine to hardcode this ID since it should be the same for all Shopware 6 Shops afaik).



*GET - http://sw6.local/api/state-machine*

Result:
{noformat}{
    "data": [
        [...]
        {
            "id": "1390ffedbd18497f886ac105380ed899",
            "type": "state_machine",
            "attributes": {
                "technicalName": "order.state",
                "name": "Order state",
                "customFields": null,
                "initialStateId": "8a6c9c11ad7d464da9f8ab264bc9d2c4",
                "createdAt": "2023-09-04T12:57:13.324+00:00",
                "updatedAt": null,
                "translated": {
                    "name": "Order state",
                    "customFields": {}
                },
                "apiAlias": "state_machine_foreign_keys_extension"
            },
            "links": {
                "self": "http://sw6.local/api/state-machine/1390ffedbd18497f886ac105380ed899"
            },
            "relationships": {
                "states": {
                    "data": [],
                    "links": {
                        "related": "http://sw6.local/api/state-machine/1390ffedbd18497f886ac105380ed899/states"
                    }
                },
                "transitions": {
                    "data": [],
                    "links": {
                        "related": "http://sw6.local/api/state-machine/1390ffedbd18497f886ac105380ed899/transitions"
                    }
                },
                "translations": {
                    "data": [],
                    "links": {
                        "related": "http://sw6.local/api/state-machine/1390ffedbd18497f886ac105380ed899/translations"
                    }
                },
                "historyEntries": {
                    "data": [],
                    "links": {
                        "related": "http://sw6.local/api/state-machine/1390ffedbd18497f886ac105380ed899/history-entries"
                    }
                }
            },
            "meta": null
        }
    ],
    [...]
}{noformat}

After getting all state machines from the API, we need to get the all states of the machine state with the technical name {{order.state}} using the following request:

*GET - /api/state-machine/1390ffedbd18497f886ac105380ed899/states*

Result:
{noformat}{
    "data": [
        [...]
        {
            "id": "b954abeca73947d4b7f3e7ad3534e381",
            "type": "state_machine_state",
            "attributes": {
                "technicalName": "cancelled",
                "name": "Cancelled",
                "stateMachineId": "1390ffedbd18497f886ac105380ed899",
                "customFields": null,
                "createdAt": "2023-09-04T12:57:13.355+00:00",
                "updatedAt": null,
                "translated": {
                    "name": "Cancelled",
                    "customFields": {}
                },
                "apiAlias": "state_machine_state_foreign_keys_extension"
            },
            [...]
        },
        [...]
    ],
    [...]
}{noformat}

column31:
Since there is no authorization set in the planner and the backend callback url can’t be completely open, it was decided to use the planner {{output_param}} to send encrypted code that will contain {{productId}} and some verification code. The encrypted code contains special characters and planner reject those. After short discussion with Nico we agreed to url_encode the code and and {{% }} character to the filter.

*What needs to be done?*

In [`planner_flask.py`|https://github.com/pmagentur/hsu-planner/blob/development/src/planner_flask.py] we need to change the filter regex 

from: 

{{VALID_FILENAME_REGEX = re.compile(r'^[a-zA-Z0-9._-]+$')}}

to:

{{VALID_FILENAME_REGEX = re.compile(r'^[a-zA-Z0-9.%_-]+$')}}

And than redeploy the new build on dev and stage

Story: [https://pmsoftware.atlassian.net/browse/HSU-183|https://pmsoftware.atlassian.net/browse/HSU-183|smart-link] 

Design: [https://xd.adobe.com/view/56536612-ea4f-49fd-bb2f-15996f3f6296-0300/|https://xd.adobe.com/view/56536612-ea4f-49fd-bb2f-15996f3f6296-0300/|smart-link] 

